[Music]  video games those words mean a lot to a  lot of different people to some a video  game is a unique way of storytelling  others play games for the competition in  camaraderie but no matter what camp  you're in it's impossible to ignore the  importance of gameplay in most cases a  game needs to be fun before anything  else it needs to feel good in the  players hands it's truly an art form and  sometimes it's an art form that gets  lost in the realistic graphics or dense  skill trees and progression systems I  recently played a top-down twin-stick  shooter that focuses almost solely on  gameplay called next machina due out in  2017  [Music]  based on what I played the game is  rather simple you jump from arena to  arena gunning down robots using the  right stick you have a special attack  and a dash but that's about it it only  takes seconds to get the hang of the  controls and within minutes next machina  had sunk its teeth in me soon I was  stringing together dashes and attacks  and formulating my own strategies on how  to survive each stage but as you'd  expect you can't just stick to one  strategy certain enemies have different  attack patterns that force me to  navigate the area in a way I hadn't  before much like resogun one of house  marks previous games timing my dashes  while slipping through enemy fire became  just as important as taking down the  opposition  but all this isn't new and how smart  where's its inspiration on its sleeve  robotron 2084 a game by Eugene Jarvis  was one of the first games to popularize  the use of twin joysticks and that came  out in 1982 a time when a good game was  one that played well and story and  presentation came second and robotron  2084 did just that the game is widely  considered one of the best arcade games  ever made and those who have fond  memories of Robotron 2084 or smash TV  will be pleased to know that this game  is being developed alongside Eugene  Jarvis next machina even shares a  similar premise to robotron 2084 robots  have taken over the world and mankind is  fighting back  so the premise is simple the controls  are simple and the mechanics are simple  those who played house marks last game  alienation might be a little  disappointed to hear this alienation had  loot customizable weapons and a deep  skill tree alienation felt more like a  Diablo twin stick shooter next machina  on the other hand favors a more arcadey  pick up and play style you won't be  showing off your loot to your friends  you'll be fighting them for high schools  but shedding complexity can also leave  the core of the game vulnerable imagine  an assassin's creed game but stripped  away the story progression and  exploration and left only the combat  that wouldn't be fun now that's an  extreme example but when you think about  a game like resogun or next machina the  game playing combat or all that's there  who's going to want to chase high scores  on a game that isn't fun well next  machina is really fun once you find a  rhythm it's intoxicating the fluid  gameplay paired with the sights and  sounds demanded my attention  [Music]  and I wasn't playing to unlock the next  level or to get a new weapon or perk I  was playing because the simple act of  playing was so damn funny obviously I  only got to see a small slice of the  full game and there could be an unlock  system or a skill tree it may even get  old after a few hours but what I can say  is that I can't remember the last time I  was so engrossed in a video game  Jarvis's and house marks idea behind the  game you probably guessed it by now is a  simple one Jarvis told house mark that  the reason humans and robots don't get  along is because humans strive for  perfection so they make robots to be  perfect while robots strive for  imperfection because they want to be  more like humans this strive for  perfection can be seen clear as day an  ex machina gameplay

